## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical, beginner-friendly security thinking around user-generated content in a chat app; it covers validation, malware scanning, storage isolation, and user feedback.

## Key Concepts
- Input validation: MIME vs extension, size caps
- Safe storage: per-user paths, UUIDs, signed URLs
- Malware scanning: pre-publish check and quarantine
- Access control and auditing: per-object ACLs, immutable logs

## Code Example
```javascript
const MAX_SIZE = 5 * 1024 * 1024; // 5 MB
const ALLOWED = new Set(['image/png','image/jpeg','application/pdf']);
app.post('/upload', (req,res)=>{
  const f = req.file;
  if (!f || f.size > MAX_SIZE) return res.status(400).send('Invalid size');
  if (!ALLOWED.has(f.mimetype)) return res.status(400).send('Unsupported type');
  const key = `uploads/${user.id}/${uuid()}${ext(f.originalname)}`;
  // upload to storage, then scan
  storage.put(key, f.buffer).then(()=> scanService.scan(key)).then(r=>{
    if (r.clean) return res.json({url: signer.sign(key)});
    // on fail
    storage.delete(key); res.status(400).send('malware detected');
  }).catch(()=> res.status(500).send('error'));
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle scalable malware scanning for peak traffic?
- What changes would you make for privacy when scanning contents?
- How would you implement a rollback path if a user re-uploads a clean file after a scan failure?

## Why This Is Asked
Real-time trading platforms demand ultra-low latency combined with robust replay protection across distributed microservices. Token binding mechanisms reduce impersonation risks while maintaining performance requirements critical to trading operations.

## Key Concepts
- Token binding and mutual TLS (mTLS) for connection-specific authentication
- Short time-to-live (TTL) tokens with audience-scoping for service isolation
- WebSocket connection nonces and replay prevention mechanisms
- Global revocation propagation strategies
- Comprehensive observability and audit trail implementation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: validate token and bind to nonce
function authorize(token, nonce, ctx){
  if (!isValidToken(token)) return false;
  if (!bindsToTLS(token, ctx)) return false;
  const session = createSecureSession(token, nonce);
  return session.isValid() && !isReplayAttempt(nonce);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a beginner's practical handling of untrusted input, safe logging, and data leakage risk in a common feature.

## Key Concepts

- JSON schema validation with no extraProperties
- Safe rendering with HTML escaping
- Logging hygiene and redaction
- Parameterized SQL queries and minimal data retention
- Basic test coverage for invalid input and log safety

## Code Example

```javascript
const Ajv = require("ajv");
const ajv = new Ajv();
const schema = {
  type: "object",
  properties: {
    message: { type: "string" },
    meta: { type: "object", properties: {}, additionalProperties: false }
  },
  required: ["message"],
  additionalProperties: false
};
if (!ajv.validate(schema, req.body)) {
  res.status(400).send("Invalid payload");
  return;
}
function sanitize(s){
  return String(s)
    .replace(/&/g, "&amp;")
    .replace(/</g, "&lt;")
    .replace(/>/g, "&gt;")
    .replace(/"/g, "&quot;")
    .replace(/'/g, "&#39;");
}
const safeMessage = sanitize(req.body.message);
db.query("INSERT INTO messages (message) VALUES ($1)", [safeMessage]);
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test for edge cases like extremely large JSON or nested payloads?
- How would you adapt this plan for real-time content rendering in a multi-tenant app?

## Why This Is Asked

Evaluates ability to design secure edge plugin architecture under real constraints: isolation, signing, attestation, auditability, and incident response.

## Key Concepts

- Edge security and sandboxing (WASM vs microVM)
- Per-tenant isolation and policy versioning
- Hardware-backed signing and remote attestation
- Tamper-evident logs and auditability
- Incident response and rollback planning

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-node example: verify plugin signature with a public key
const crypto = require('crypto');
function verify(sig, data, pubKey) {
  const verify = crypto.createVerify('SHA256');
  verify.update(data);
  return verify.verify(pubKey, sig);
}
```

